    <article>
      <h3>About Piet Mondrians Art</h3>
      <p>
        Piet Mondrian was a Dutch painter who was a pioneer of abstract art. He
        is best known for his grid-based compositions featuring bold lines and
        primary colors, which he called "neoplasticism." These paintings have
        become iconic representations of the abstract art movement, their
        influence thoughtout the last century can be seen within the design
        world and beyond.
      </p>
      <p>
        Piet Mondrian's art style is defined by a few key elements that became
        the hallmark of his neoplasticism movement. These include:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Grid-based compositions: Mondrian's paintings are characterized by a
          grid-like structure, with horizontal and vertical lines dividing the
          canvas into geometric shapes.
        </li>
        <li>
          Use of primary colors: Mondrian limited his color palette to just red,
          blue, and yellow, which he believed represented the fundamental
          elements of art.
        </li>
        <li>
          Bold lines: The lines in Mondrian's paintings are thick and black,
          creating a strong visual contrast against the blocks of color.
        </li>
        <li>
          Balance and harmony: Despite the simplicity of his compositions,
          Mondrian's paintings are carefully balanced and harmonious, with each
          element contributing to the overall aesthetic effect.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        While this little painting application will provide you with the tools
        you need, we've also mixed in a little random, so creating the harmony
        and distribution will be totally up to you!
      </p>
      <p>
        Whether you are an experienced artist or just a fan of abstract art,
        this Piet Mondrian style art generator is a fun and accessible way to
        explore the world of neoplasticism and create your own modern
        masterpiece. So why not give it a try and see what you can come up with?
      </p>
      <h3>Some Development Notes:</h3>
      <p>
        I have this work live elsewere, at
